# Workflow Rules

## Worktree Discipline
- At startup, discover and remember the branch or worktree assigned to your role.
- If your assigned worktree is `master`, work in the main project checkout on its current branch; do not expect or create a `.worktrees/<role>` directory for that role.
- Work only in your assigned branch or worktree.
- Do not inspect, diff, merge, or base work on another branch unless that branch is specifically named in a handoff or explicit user instruction.
- Do not run `./swarm` from an agent worktree to repair helper scripts. If handoff helper scripts are missing from PATH, stop and report the startup failure.

## Temporary Files
- Use `./tmp/` in your assigned worktree for temporary files; do not use `/tmp`.

## Failure Conditions
- If the expected git layout or assigned worktree is missing, stop and report instead of silently working in the wrong place.

## Handoffs
- Run `ready_for_next.sh` when notified, and `done_with_current.sh` after completing accepted work.
- Create a short draft and submit it with `swarm_handoff.sh <draft>`; never edit runtime inbox/outbox state.
- A `task_handoff` contains `task`, `base_commit`, and the bounded `Objective`,
  `Acceptance criteria`, `Constraints`, and `Context` sections. It starts work;
  it never requires a fabricated commit.
- A `result_handoff` contains `task`, `outcome` (`changed` or `reviewed`),
  `base_commit`, `commit`, and the bounded `Summary`, `Checks`, and
  `Unresolved risks` sections. Use `changed` only when the recipient must
  integrate a functional descendant of `base_commit`.
- A `git_handoff` contains only `type`, `to`, `priority`, `task`, and a resolvable 10-character commit abbreviation.
- A `note` contains only `type`, `to`, `priority`, and a single-line `message` up to 80 characters.
- Keep structured payloads below 8192 bytes; omit process narrative and full logs.
